      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the probability that both the two-digit number \\( ab \\) and the digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are all divisible by 3 when two fair 6-sided dice are tossed, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the conditions for divisibility by 3:**\n   - A number \\( ab \\) is divisible by 3 if the sum \\( a + b \\) is divisible by 3.\n   - The digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are divisible by 3 if \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are 3 or 6 (since these are the only digits between 1 and 6 that are divisible by 3).\n\n2. **List the possible outcomes for \\( a \\) and \\( b \\):**\n   - \\( a \\) can be any of the digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6 (6 choices).\n   - \\( b \\) can be any of the digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6, but to satisfy the condition that \\( a + b \\) is divisible by 3, \\( b \\) must be one of the digits 3, 6 (since 1, 4, and 7 are not divisible by 3 when added to 3 or 6).\n\n3. **Count the favorable outcomes:**\n   - For each value of \\( a \\), \\( b \\) can be chosen in 2 ways (3 or 6).\n   - Therefore, the total number of favorable outcomes is \\( 6 \\times 2 = 12 \\).\n\n4. **Count the total possible outcomes:**\n   - Each die can land on any of 6 faces, so there are \\( 6 \\times 6 = 36 \\) total possible outcomes when two dice are tossed.\n\n5. **Calculate the probability:**\n   - The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of possible outcomes}} = \\frac{12}{36} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the probability that both the two-digit number \\( ab \\) and the digits \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are all divisible by 3 is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\\).",
